\begin_layout Standard
gSPI Command Structure:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
C command:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
0 Read.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
1 Write.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A access:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
0 Fixed address.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
1 Incremental address.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Function (F1,
 F2):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
00 Func 0,
 All SPI specific registers
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
01 Func 1,
 Registers and memories belonging to other blocks in the chip (64 bytes max).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
10 Func 2,
 DMA channel 1.
 WLAN packets up to 2048 bytes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
11 Func 3,
 DMA channel 2 (optional).
 Packets up to 2048 bytes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Write
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The host puts the first bit of the data onto the bus half a clock-cycle before the first active edge following the CS going low.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The following bits are clocked out on the falling edge of the gSPI clock.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The device samples the data on the active edge.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Write/Read
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The host reads on the rising edge of the clock requiring data from the device to be made available before the first rising-clock edge of the data.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The last clock edge of the fixed delay word can be used to represent the first bit of the following data word.
 This allows data to be ready for the first clock edge without relying on asynchronous delays.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Read
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
The read command always follows a separate write to set up the WLAN device for a read.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
This command differs from the write/read command in the following respects:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Labeling
\labelwidthstring 00.00.0000
a) Chip select goes high between the command/address and the data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Labeling
\labelwidthstring 00.00.0000
b) The time interval between the command/address is not fixed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Status
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The gSPI interface supports status notification to the host after a read/write transaction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This status notification provides information about packet errors,
 protocol errors,
 available packets in the RX queue,
 etc.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The status information helps reduce the number of interrupts to the host.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The status-reporting feature can be switched off using a register bit without any timing overhead.
\end_layout

