\begin_layout Section
Boot Sequence
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
gSPI Host-Device Handshake
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To initiate communication through the gSPI after power-up the host needs to bring up the WLAN chip by writing to the wake-up WLAN register bit.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Writing 1 to this bit will start up the necessary crystals and PLLs so that the CYW43439 is ready for data transfer.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The device can signal an interrupt to the host indicating that the device is awake and ready.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This procedure also must be followed for waking up the device in sleep mode.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The device can interrupt the host using the WLAN IRQ line whenever it has any information to pass to the host.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On getting an interrupt the host needs to read the interrupt and/or status register to determine the cause of the interrupt and then take necessary actions.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Boot-Up Sequence
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After power-up the gSPI host must wait 50 ms for the device to be out of reset.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For this the host needs to poll with a read command to F0 address 0x14.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Address 0x14 contains a predefined bit pattern:
 0xFEEDBEAD.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As soon as the host gets a response back with the correct register content it implies that the device has powered up and is out of reset.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After that,
 the host needs to set the wake-up WLAN bit (F0 reg 0x00 bit 7).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Wake-up WLAN turns the PLL on;
 however,
 the PLL doesn’t lock until the host programs the PLL registers to set the crystal frequency.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the first time after power-up the host needs to wait for the availability of the low-power clock inside the device.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Once it is available the host needs to write to a PMU register to set the crystal frequency.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This will turn on the PLL.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After the PLL is locked a chipActive interrupt is issued to the host.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This indicates device awake/ready status.
 
\end_layout

